{"name":"Classic Berry","hex":"#7c5261","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"classic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to the first class or rank, especially in literature or art.","Exemplary of a particular style; defining a class or category; typical; archetypical; epitomic."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A perfect and/or early example of a particular style.","An artistic work of lasting worth, such as a film or song; a work of enduring excellence."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/classic"},{"term":"berry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small succulent fruit, of any one of many varieties.","A soft fruit which develops from a single ovary and contains seeds not encased in pits."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To pick berries.","To bear or produce berries."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/berry"}],"entries":[{"title":"Classic","blocks":[{"text":"A classic is an outstanding example of a particular style; something of lasting worth or with a timeless quality; of the first or highest quality, class, or rank – something that exemplifies its class. The word can be an adjective or a noun. It denotes a particular quality in art, architecture, literature, design, technology, or other cultural artifacts. In commerce, products are named 'classic' to denote a long-standing popular version or model, to distinguish it from a newer variety. Classic is used to describe many major, long-standing sporting events. Colloquially, an everyday occurrence may be described in some dialects of English as 'an absolute classic'."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Classic"},{"title":"Berry","blocks":[{"text":"A berry is a small, pulpy, and often edible fruit. Typically, berries are juicy, rounded, brightly colored, sweet, sour or tart, and do not have a stone or pit although many pips or seeds may be present.‍ Common examples of berries in the culinary sense are strawberries, raspberries, blueberries, blackberries, white currants, blackcurrants, and redcurrants.‍ In Britain, soft fruit is a horticultural term for such fruits.‍"}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Berberis_thunbergii_berries.jpg/330px-Berberis_thunbergii_berries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Norwegian_blueberry.jpg/250px-Norwegian_blueberry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Norwegian_blueberry.jpg/500px-Norwegian_blueberry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Norwegian_blueberry.jpg/250px-Norwegian_blueberry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANorwegian_blueberry.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Ribes_rubrum2005-07-17.JPG/250px-Ribes_rubrum2005-07-17.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Ribes_rubrum2005-07-17.JPG/330px-Ribes_rubrum2005-07-17.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Ribes_rubrum2005-07-17.JPG/250px-Ribes_rubrum2005-07-17.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARibes_rubrum2005-07-17.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Lonicera_coerulea_a3.jpg/250px-Lonicera_coerulea_a3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Lonicera